  "summary": "New preprints audit STATE's decoder, expose plate-layout confounds in screen benchmarks, and propose context-aware models for unseen perturbations.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is predicting single-cell perturbation responses and questioning when those predictions can be trusted. An audit of the STATE state-transition decoder reports that a low-rank attention bottleneck bounds reliability. Separately, an analysis of perturbation screens shows that plate layout and well effects confound both donor variation and benchmark evaluation, and it proposes design-aware estimators. Several methods aim at generalization to unmeasured conditions by modeling experimental context explicitly. RELAY uses relational in-context learning over measured control–perturbed pairs that share experimental context. UnDetect separates cellular context from compound effects and fuses structure, targets, mechanisms and Cell Painting morphology in a multi-view kernel to handle unseen compounds. CellCanon extends this toward an agentic world model combining prediction, evidence-grounded explanation and observation-driven updating. What is new is a dual focus: diagnosing architectural and experimental-design limits of current evaluations, alongside context-conditioned prediction intended to make transfer to new perturbations more reliable.",
